{
  "dom_id": "step-e214",
  "name": "Example: Calculating the Speed of a Particle at a Particular Moment",
  "problem": "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t) = t^2 - 36t,\\quad t\\geq 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle when $t=1\\,\\textrm{s}$.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-36t) \\\\  & =2t-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=1,$ the velocity is\n\n$$\nv(1) = 2(1) - 36 = -34.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed is $|{-34}| = 34\\, \\textrm{m/s}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
